1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
The TCP window field is sixteen bits, so the largest value it can carry is 65,535 bytes.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
A window is bytes in flight, so window divided by round-trip time is the throughput ceiling.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
On a 100 ms path the ceiling is 5,242,800 bits per second, about 5.24 Mbit/s, whatever the link can do.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The ceiling is a property of the header, not of the network: a faster link or a longer path makes it worse.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Bandwidth times delay is the amount of data the path can hold: the bandwidth-delay product.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
At 1 Gbit/s over 100 ms the pipe holds 12,500,000 bytes, and 65,535 bytes is about one two-hundredth of it.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Three links, three required windows: 250,000 bytes, 12,500,000 bytes and 62,500,000 bytes.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
So the question the extension answers is: how do you name a window larger than 65,535 using a sixteen-bit field?

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The window scale option carries one number, a shift count, and the true window is the field shifted left by it.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Shift 0 keeps 65,535 bytes; shift 8 reaches 16,776,960; shift 14 reaches 1,073,725,440 bytes, about 1 GiB.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The top of the range is 2^30 minus 2^14, which is 16,384 bytes short of exactly one gibibyte.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
At shift 14 and a 100 ms path the ceiling becomes 85,898,035,200 bits per second, about 85.9 Gbit/s.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
TCP decides old from new by asking whether a sequence number is within 2^31 of the left edge of the window.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The sender and receiver windows can be out of phase by one window, so twice the maximum window must stay below 2^31.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Shift 14 gives 2,147,450,880 for twice the window, which clears 2^31 by 32,768 bytes; shift 15 overshoots it.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
So 14 is the largest shift that works, and a peer that sends more must be read as 14.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The option is sent in the handshake only, and both sides must send it for scaling to be used in either direction.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The window field of a SYN segment is never scaled, so the 65,535 in the handshake is 65,535 and not 8,388,480.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Two hosts with buffers of 8,000,000 and 1,000,000 bytes choose shifts 7 and 4.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Both then advertise the same field value, 62,500, and it means 8,000,000 bytes from one side and 1,000,000 from the other.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Each connection keeps two shift counters: the one you send your own window with, and the one you read the peer's with.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The receiver right-shifts what it sends, and the sender left-shifts what it receives, by the peer's count.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The first host reads 62,500 with its own shift 7 and gets 8,000,000, eight times the true 1,000,000.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The scale applies to the window field only: the congestion window is kept as a local number and is never scaled.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The shift is chosen from the receive buffer before the connection opens: the smallest count whose maximum window covers it.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
A one-mebibyte buffer looks like shift 4, since 2^16 is 65,536, but shift 4 reaches only 1,048,560 bytes.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
So the answer is 5, and the general rule is the smallest count with 65,535 times 2^s at least the buffer.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
A four-gigabyte buffer would need shift 17, which is clamped to 14, so only 1,073,725,440 bytes can be advertised.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The timestamps option is ten bytes: a kind, a length, a four-byte value and a four-byte echo.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
It takes the header from 20 to 30 bytes, and with window scaling too, to 34; payload efficiency falls from 98.65 to 97.72 per cent.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The difference between the current clock and an echoed value is a round-trip sample, taken from almost every segment.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The echo is one saved value per connection, updated only when the incoming value is at least it and the segment is within the last acknowledged byte.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A connection at a gigabit per second uses up the whole sequence space in about 34 seconds, so old and new can collide.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The test is modular: an arrival is older when the saved value minus it, modulo 2^32, lies between 1 and 2^31.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
A value 1,000 behind the saved 5,000,000 gives a difference of 1,000 and is rejected; 100 ahead gives 4,294,967,196 and is accepted.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
After the clock wraps the test still works, and a value saved before a wrap is discarded after 24 days of silence.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Without timestamps a retransmission makes the next acknowledgement untimeable, which is why the sample is refused.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
With timestamps the echo names the copy: an echo of 1400 measures 100 ms, an echo of 1000 measures 500 ms.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
One guard remains: an echo may update the estimate only when the segment advances the left edge of the send window.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The five slips: a scaled SYN, the wrong shift, a divided buffer, a signed comparison, and a sample that acknowledges nothing.

